# Test: a FlowSpec rule recorded by a run that ended without withdrawing it, and
# not withdrawn by the next start, is reported by `ze doctor` with the victim and
# the plugin restart as its fix; once the cause of the failed withdrawal is
# corrected, the fix doctor printed withdraws the rule, removes the record, and
# clears the report.
#
# QUESTION: when the daemon holds a FlowSpec record that its running responder
#           does not own, does `ze doctor` through the daemon name it under
#           doctor-ddos-flowspec-orphan-announcement, with the victim prefix and
#           `ze request plugin restart ddos-flowspec` as the fix, and does
#           running that fix put the withdrawal on a peer's wire and leave
#           doctor reporting nothing? A unit test cannot decide it: the record
#           crosses a daemon death in the disk store, the report travels from
#           the plugin's runtime check through the daemon's doctor, and the
#           restart is the daemon's own (handlePluginRestart -> restartPlugin).
#
# VALIDATES: spec-single-owner-lifecycle-5-session-end-locals AC-16 (the runtime
#            half: "a start-time withdrawal that failed", and "the report clears
#            when the record is removed"), user story 5, and the check's Fix
#            (ai/rules/user-facing-errors.md, "the exact command that fixes
#            it"): the command is taken from doctor's own output and run, never
#            retyped.
# PREVENTS:  a recorded rule invisible to `ze doctor`, and a fix that leaves the
#            report standing after the operator ran it.
#
# HOW THE DAEMON REACHES THE STATE, with nothing written by hand:
#   1. The first daemon announces the blackhole-fallback rule for the remote
#      victim, and the plugin records it (responder.go announce -> Record). The
#      flood driver waits for the record itself, read back through the daemon
#      (`show data cat`): the live flag is published before the record is
#      written, so it is no barrier.
#   2. The first daemon is KILLED. A killed process withdraws nothing, and the
#      record stays in the disk store (docs/guide/ddos-mitigation.md, "What
#      each end of the plugin does"). A SIGTERM would leave the record too
#      for this in-process plugin, because the daemon's stop refuses its
#      calls before its run ends (register.go stopResponder), but the kill
#      is the end that leaves the record whatever the placement: a forked
#      plugin signalled before the daemon's stop reaches it withdraws the
#      rule and removes the record.
#   3. The second daemon starts on a config that keeps `ddos flowspec` but
#      whose peer no longer attaches ddos-flowspec. The start-time withdrawal
#      (withdrawRecordedAnnouncement) is refused by the send permission
#      (reactor/send_permission.go filterPermittedPeers), so the record stays
#      and the running responder announces nothing: the runtime check
#      (doctor.go answerOrphanRuntimeCheck) reports it.
#   4. The cause is corrected: the operator restores the attach block
#      (fix-cause.sh copies running.conf over running2.conf and asks for a
#      reload). A changed attach block travels through RemovePeer and AddPeer
#      (reactor/delivery_graph.go), so the session to the peer restarts as
#      connection 3, and the script waits until it is established: a
#      withdrawal sent before that reaches no peer and fails the same way.
#   5. The fix doctor printed is run as printed (run-fix.sh). The restarted
#      plugin's start sends the withdrawal of the recorded rule through the
#      restored attach, the engine accepts it, and the plugin removes the record
#      (withdrawRecordedAnnouncement); doctor then reports nothing
#      (plugin/ddos-flowspec-orphan-cleared). The engine writes nothing to
#      connection 3, because that session never carried the rule
#      (reactor_api_batch.go splitOnAdvertised, logWithdrawWithheld). RFC 4271
#      Section 4.3 scopes a withdrawn route to one connection:
#        "Each such route is identified by its destination
#        (expressed as an IP prefix), which unambiguously identifies the route
#        in the context of the BGP speaker - BGP speaker connection to which
#        it has been previously advertised."
#
# WHAT THE PEER ASSERTS. Connection 1 (first daemon) receives the announcement
# of 203.0.113.9/32 with traffic-rate 0. Connection 2 (second daemon, no attach)
# receives no FlowSpec UPDATE for the victim (two scoped rejects): the refused
# start-time withdrawal sends nothing. It ends with the Cease, subcode 6 (Other
# Configuration Change), that the reload restoring the attach block causes.
# Connection 3 (the session the reload restarted) receives Ze's End-of-RIB and
# no UPDATE for the victim, announced or withdrawn (two scoped rejects): it
# never carried the rule, so the withdrawal is withheld from it (item 5).
#
# WHAT THIS PROVES ABOUT THE FIX: the withdrawal reaches the engine (the
# daemon logs `withdrawal withheld: this session has advertised no route to
# the peer`), nothing is sent on a session that never carried the rule, the
# record is removed, and doctor clears. It cannot prove a withdrawal on the
# wire: that needs the session that carried the rule, which the kill ended.
# The wire halves are TestRestartWithdrawsTheOrphanAndClearsTheReport
# (internal/plugins/ddos/flowspec/doctor_test.go) and the section-removed .ci.
#
# RED PROOF: change the "del" in withdrawRecordedRule (responder.go) to "add".
# The restarted plugin then announces 203.0.113.9/32 on connection 3, which is
# established and attached, and reject=bgp:conn=3:scope=announced fires.
#
# WHY THE DETECTION IS CRITICAL. With characterize-enable false the blackhole
# fallback is the only announce path, and onDetected takes it at critical only.
# The grade is set once, when the attack activates (detector.go onAttackStart:
# GradeSeverity of peakRxPps against the threshold), and is never revised.
# peakRxPps is the highest one-second sample above the threshold since the last
# clear, and the threshold is the absolute-floor of 100 pps on the quiet
# baseline. confirm-duration 2 makes the second graded sample a whole second
# inside the steady flood (fixture ddosFlowspecSteadyFlood, 4000-packet bursts
# every 250 ms), and critical needs only 500 pps in it. Both daemons' configs
# keep these leaves identical to the parent-removed draft's.
#
# STATUS: a Linux/QEMU integration test; NOT executable on the darwin dev host.
#   net-admin builds the transit veth pair; bpf lets traffic-usage name the
#   victim (internal/plugins/trafficusage/attach_linux.go).
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,bpf
option=exclusive:group=ddos-flood
# The record has to survive the first daemon: the memory store dies with it.
option=storage:value=disk
option=timeout:value=180s
option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.flowspec:value=info
option=env:var=ze.ssh.username:value=admin
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass
option=env:var=ze.ssh.port:value=$PORT2

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=3
option=linger:value=true
# First daemon: the announcement of 203.0.113.9/32, traffic-rate 0 (discard).
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:announced=060120CB007109:attribute=16,8006000000000000
# Second daemon, no attach: the reload that restores the attach block restarts
# the peer (restartPeer), which ends this session with RFC 4486 Section 4 Cease
# subcode 6, Other Configuration Change.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0015030606
# The refused start-time withdrawal reaches no peer, and nothing announces the
# rule on this session either.
reject=bgp:conn=2:scope=announced:pattern=060120CB007109
reject=bgp:conn=2:scope=withdrawn:pattern=060120CB007109
# Second daemon, after the reload restored the attach. Ze sends an End-of-RIB
# for every negotiated family at establishment (peer_initial_sync.go
# sendEORFamilies; no manual-eor here), and both OPENs carry ipv4/unicast, so
# the ipv4/unicast End-of-RIB is certain. It is what carries the peer onto
# this connection; the ipv4/flow one is tolerated as an End-of-RIB.
expect=bgp:conn=3:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
# This session never carried the rule, so the fix's withdrawal is withheld
# from it, and nothing announces the rule here either.
reject=bgp:conn=3:scope=announced:pattern=060120CB007109
reject=bgp:conn=3:scope=withdrawn:pattern=060120CB007109
EOF_PEER

tmpfs=running.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	authentication {
		# bcrypt cost-4 hash of "testpass".
		user admin {
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
		}
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT2;
		}
	}
}
ddos {
	detect {
		enabled true
		absolute-floor 100
		confirm-duration 2
		clear-consecutive-checks 100
		startup-grace 0
		baseline-window 10
		check-interval 1
		characterize-enable false
	}
	observe {
		incident-ring-size 100
	}
	flowspec {
		response-level enforce
		action discard
		blackhole-fallback true
		max-mitigation-duration 3600
		hold-down 3600
	}
}
traffic {
	usage {
		enabled true
		track-ip true
		interfaces {
			interface zdd0p {
				enabled true
			}
			interface zdd0 {
				enabled true
			}
		}
	}
}
bgp {
	filter {
		import {
			final accept
		}
		export {
			final accept
		}
	}
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.2
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
				ipv4/flow { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		attach process ddos-flowspec {
			send [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# The second daemon's config: running.conf with the peer's attach block
# removed, and nothing else changed.
tmpfs=running2.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF2
system {
